---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Hormones & Obesity: Why the Body Defends Its Fat — Lifelong Athletics"
  description="Your fat cells signal how much fuel is stored — in obesity that message stops getting through. Leptin, insulin, thyroid, metabolic adaptation, and what to put on the plate, explained plainly."
>

<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz,wght@12..96,500;12..96,700;12..96,800&family=Newsreader:ital,opsz,wght@0,6..72,300;0,6..72,400;0,6..72,500;1,6..72,400&family=Space+Mono:wght@400;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />

<div class="ho-wrap">

  <div class="ho-backbar"><a href="/blog" class="ho-back">← Back to Education</a></div>

  <!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hormones &amp; obesity — explained plainly</p>
      <h1>Why your body<br>defends its fat<em>and fights back.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Your fat cells keep a running note of how much fuel is stored, and they send it to your brain. In obesity, that message stops getting through. Almost everything else — the hunger, the regain, the slowed engine — follows from that one lost signal.</p>

      <div class="thread">
        <div class="thread-head"><span class="dot"></span> Body &rarr; Brain &nbsp;·&nbsp; live signal</div>
        <div class="thread-cols">
          <div class="thread-col">
            <p class="col-label">When it works</p>
            <div class="bubble b1">Fat cells here. Storage is full. You can stop eating now.</div>
            <div class="bubble reply b2">Got it. Turning hunger down.<span class="stamp ok">✓ Delivered</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="thread-col">
            <p class="col-label">When it doesn't</p>
            <div class="bubble b3">Fat cells here. Storage is <em>very</em> full. Please stop eating.</div>
            <div class="bubble dead b4">…<span class="stamp bad">✕ Not delivered · brain still hungry</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="byline">The message that never arrived — leptin, insulin, thyroid, and what to put on the plate</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 1. WHAT IS A HORMONE ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 — The basics</p>
      <h2>A hormone is just a tiny messenger</h2>
      <p>Your body has trillions of cells. They cannot shout across the room to each other. So they send messages instead.</p>
      <p>A <span class="big-word">hormone</span> is one of those messages. It is made in one part of the body, it rides through the blood like a letter in the post, and it is read somewhere else. That is the whole idea.</p>
      <p>Four of these messengers matter a lot for body weight. Here they are, in the simplest words possible.</p>

      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Messenger 01</p>
          <h4>Insulin</h4>
          <p>The <strong>key</strong>. After you eat, sugar floats in your blood. Insulin unlocks your cells so the sugar can get inside and be used.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Messenger 02</p>
          <h4>Leptin</h4>
          <p>The <strong>fuel gauge</strong>. Made by fat cells. It tells the brain how much energy is stored, so the brain knows when to stop being hungry.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Messenger 03</p>
          <h4>Thyroid hormone</h4>
          <p>The <strong>thermostat</strong>. It sets how fast your body burns fuel while you are just sitting still doing nothing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 2. THE BROKEN GAUGE ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 — The central problem</p>
      <h2>The tank is full. The gauge says empty.</h2>
      <p>Fat cells make leptin. More fat cells means more leptin. So in a bigger body, there is a <em>lot</em> of leptin floating around.</p>
      <p>You would expect that person to feel very full all the time. The opposite happens. The brain stops listening to leptin. Scientists call this <span class="big-word">leptin resistance</span>.</p>
      <p>Here is the part worth sitting with. It is not that the tank is empty. It is that the <strong>wire between the gauge and the dashboard is broken</strong>. The tank is overflowing, and the dashboard still reads empty.</p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 260" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a full fuel tank connected by a broken wire to a gauge reading empty">
          <!-- tank -->
          <rect x="34" y="42" width="96" height="160" rx="16" fill="none" stroke="#DAD3EC" stroke-width="3"/>
          <rect x="42" y="62" width="80" height="132" rx="10" fill="#12A594" opacity=".85"/>
          <text x="82" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="15" fill="#fff" letter-spacing="1">FULL</text>
          <text x="82" y="228" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="1.6">FAT STORES</text>

          <!-- broken wire -->
          <path d="M136 122 L246 122" stroke="#241B36" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <path d="M300 122 L392 122" stroke="#241B36" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-dasharray="7 9" opacity=".35"/>
          <path d="M252 104 L268 122 L252 140" stroke="#FF4F6D" stroke-width="3.5" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <path d="M294 104 L278 122 L294 140" stroke="#FF4F6D" stroke-width="3.5" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <text x="273" y="166" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#FF4F6D" letter-spacing="1.4">SIGNAL BREAKS</text>

          <!-- gauge -->
          <path d="M420 178 A 82 82 0 0 1 584 178" fill="none" stroke="#DAD3EC" stroke-width="16" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <path d="M420 178 A 82 82 0 0 1 445 120" fill="none" stroke="#FF4F6D" stroke-width="16" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <line x1="502" y1="178" x2="436" y2="150" stroke="#241B36" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <circle cx="502" cy="178" r="8" fill="#241B36"/>
          <text x="416" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="13" fill="#FF4F6D" font-weight="700">E</text>
          <text x="590" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="13" fill="#5B4E75">F</text>
          <text x="502" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="1.6">WHAT THE BRAIN READS</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>The brain is not being stubborn. It is doing exactly the right thing for the information it has been given — and the information is wrong.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>So the brain does what any sensible brain would do when it believes fuel is running out. It switches hunger on and keeps it on.</p>
      <div class="pull">The hunger is real. It is coming from a brain that has been handed a false reading.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 3. THE LOOP ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 — Why it repeats</p>
      <h2>And then it goes round again</h2>
      <p>One broken signal would be a small problem. The trouble is that it feeds itself. Each step makes the next step more likely, and after five steps you are back where you started — only heavier.</p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 560" role="img" aria-label="Circular diagram of the leptin and weight gain loop with five steps">
          <circle cx="280" cy="280" r="175" fill="none" stroke="#DAD3EC" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="3 7"/>

          <!-- arrowheads, clockwise -->
          <g fill="#FF4F6D">
            <path d="M0,-8 L13,0 L0,8 Z" transform="translate(382.9,138.4) rotate(36)"/>
            <path d="M0,-8 L13,0 L0,8 Z" transform="translate(446.4,334.1) rotate(108)"/>
            <path d="M0,-8 L13,0 L0,8 Z" transform="translate(280,455) rotate(180)"/>
            <path d="M0,-8 L13,0 L0,8 Z" transform="translate(113.6,334.1) rotate(252)"/>
            <path d="M0,-8 L13,0 L0,8 Z" transform="translate(177.1,138.4) rotate(324)"/>
          </g>

          <!-- nodes -->
          <g font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="15" text-anchor="middle" fill="#241B36">
            <circle cx="280" cy="105" r="54" fill="#FBFAFE" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <text x="280" y="100">Weight</text><text x="280" y="118">gain</text>

            <circle cx="446.4" cy="225.9" r="54" fill="#FBFAFE" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <text x="446.4" y="215">Leptin</text><text x="446.4" y="233">stops</text><text x="446.4" y="251">working</text>

            <circle cx="382.9" cy="421.6" r="54" fill="#FBFAFE" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <text x="382.9" y="416">Message</text><text x="382.9" y="434">is lost</text>

            <circle cx="177.1" cy="421.6" r="54" fill="#FBFAFE" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <text x="177.1" y="416">Hunger</text><text x="177.1" y="434">stays on</text>

            <circle cx="113.6" cy="225.9" r="54" fill="#FBFAFE" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <text x="113.6" y="215">You eat</text><text x="113.6" y="233">more than</text><text x="113.6" y="251">you need</text>
          </g>

          <!-- step badges -->
          <g font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff">
            <circle cx="280" cy="47" r="15" fill="#FF4F6D"/><text x="280" y="52">1</text>
            <circle cx="501" cy="207" r="15" fill="#FF4F6D"/><text x="501" y="212">2</text>
            <circle cx="416" cy="472" r="15" fill="#FF4F6D"/><text x="416" y="477">3</text>
            <circle cx="144" cy="472" r="15" fill="#FF4F6D"/><text x="144" y="477">4</text>
            <circle cx="59" cy="207" r="15" fill="#FF4F6D"/><text x="59" y="212">5</text>
          </g>

          <!-- centre -->
          <text x="280" y="270" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="2.4">THE LOOP</text>
          <text x="280" y="298" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-weight="800" font-size="21" fill="#241B36">no exit door</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>More fat → more leptin → the brain tunes it out → the "stop eating" message never lands → hunger stays on → more eating → more fat. Step 5 walks straight back into step 1.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 4. TABLE ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">04 — The scoreboard</p>
      <h2>Four messengers, before and after</h2>
      <p>Here is what happens to each hormone in a larger body, and what happens when weight comes off. Read the last column carefully — it is where the surprise is hiding.</p>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Messenger</th>
              <th>In obesity</th>
              <th>In plain words</th>
              <th>After weight loss</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="hname">Insulin</span><br><span class="tag up">Too high</span></td>
              <td>About 8 in 10 people have too much insulin in the blood.</td>
              <td>The locks on the cells are worn out, so the body keeps making more keys.</td>
              <td><span class="tag down">Recovers well</span><br>The locks start working again. Sugar gets into cells properly.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="hname">Thyroid</span><br><span class="tag same">Usually normal</span></td>
              <td>Hormone levels are normal. There may just be fewer "antennas" on cells to catch the signal.</td>
              <td>The thermostat itself is fine. A slow thyroid does <em>not</em> explain most weight gain.</td>
              <td><span class="tag up">Turns down</span><br>Active thyroid hormone (T3) drops. You burn fewer calories at rest.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="hname">Growth hormone</span><br><span class="tag down">Lower</span></td>
              <td>Reduced — and this may be on purpose, not a fault.</td>
              <td>One of its jobs is to pull fat out of storage. With plenty stored, the body sends less of it.</td>
              <td><span class="tag down">Back to normal</span><br>Levels return once the situation changes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="hname">Leptin</span><br><span class="tag up">High, ignored</span></td>
              <td>Lots of it, but the brain has stopped responding.</td>
              <td>The fuel gauge is shouting and nobody is listening.</td>
              <td><span class="tag up">Still off</span><br>Falls, but stays higher than in someone who never had obesity — and the brain still under-reacts.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="note">
        <p class="eyebrow">Read the pattern</p>
        <p>Insulin and growth hormone <strong>recover</strong>. Thyroid and leptin do not. Those two point in the same unhelpful direction: <strong>burning less</strong> while <strong>feeling hungrier</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 5. THE TWIST ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">05 — The twist nobody warns you about</p>
      <h2>Two people, same weight, different engines</h2>
      <p>Imagine two people. Both weigh 70&nbsp;kg today. One has always weighed 70&nbsp;kg. The other started at 90&nbsp;kg and worked hard to get here.</p>
      <p>They look the same on the scale. They are not the same underneath. The person who lost weight burns noticeably fewer calories doing nothing at all.</p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart comparing calories burned at rest between someone always at this weight and someone who lost weight to reach it">
          <line x1="70" y1="238" x2="610" y2="238" stroke="#DAD3EC" stroke-width="2"/>

          <rect x="120" y="58" width="130" height="180" rx="8" fill="#12A594"/>
          <text x="185" y="44" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-weight="800" font-size="22" fill="#241B36">100%</text>
          <text x="185" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75">ALWAYS 70 KG</text>

          <rect x="390" y="85" width="130" height="153" rx="8" fill="#FF4F6D"/>
          <text x="455" y="71" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-weight="800" font-size="22" fill="#241B36">~85%</text>
          <text x="455" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75">LOST 20 KG TO GET HERE</text>

          <path d="M262 66 L378 66" stroke="#241B36" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 5"/>
          <text x="320" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#241B36">the gap</text>
          <text x="320" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#5B4E75">roughly 10–15%</text>
          <text x="320" y="134" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#5B4E75">fewer calories</text>
          <text x="320" y="156" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#5B4E75">burned at rest</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Illustrative. Studies of people after weight loss typically find resting energy use around 10–15% below what body size alone would predict. Two causes stack up: less active thyroid hormone, and some muscle lost along with the fat.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Meanwhile the fuel gauge has not been repaired. Leptin falls when fat falls — it has to, because fat cells make it — but it does not settle back to a normal reading, and the brain still does not fully trust it.</p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of leptin levels in three situations">
          <line x1="60" y1="196" x2="612" y2="196" stroke="#DAD3EC" stroke-width="2"/>

          <rect x="92" y="150" width="112" height="46" rx="7" fill="#12A594"/>
          <text x="148" y="138" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75">NORMAL</text>
          <text x="148" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#241B36">Never had obesity</text>

          <rect x="264" y="42" width="112" height="154" rx="7" fill="#FF4F6D"/>
          <text x="320" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#FF4F6D">VERY HIGH</text>
          <text x="320" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#241B36">With obesity</text>

          <rect x="436" y="98" width="112" height="98" rx="7" fill="#F0A202"/>
          <text x="492" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="11.5" fill="#5B4E75">STILL RAISED</text>
          <text x="492" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="15" fill="#241B36">After weight loss</text>

          <text x="336" y="242" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="10.5" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="1.6">LEPTIN IN THE BLOOD — RELATIVE, NOT TO SCALE</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>The gauge gets quieter after weight loss, but it never quite returns to the reading of someone who was never in the loop.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="pull">Burning less, and feeling hungrier, at the same time. That combination has a name: metabolic adaptation. It is the reason weight comes back.</div>
      <p>This is worth saying clearly, because a lot of people blame themselves for it. Regaining weight is not a collapse of willpower. It is a body running a very old survival program that treats fat loss as a famine and fights it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 6. FOOD ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">06 — What to actually do</p>
      <h2>So what goes on the plate?</h2>
      <p>If the body's defences are hunger and a slower engine, then good food choices are the ones that <em>answer those two problems directly</em>. Keep the muscle. Keep full for longer. That is basically the whole strategy.</p>
      <p>Food gives us three big nutrients, called <span class="big-word">macros</span>. Here is roughly how a day is usually split.</p>

      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Donut chart showing a daily split of about 55 percent carbohydrate, 25 percent fat and 20 percent protein">
          <g transform="translate(160,150)">
            <circle r="80" fill="none" stroke="#F0A202" stroke-width="44"
                    stroke-dasharray="276.5 502.65" stroke-dashoffset="0" transform="rotate(-90)"/>
            <circle r="80" fill="none" stroke="#372755" stroke-width="44"
                    stroke-dasharray="125.7 502.65" stroke-dashoffset="-276.5" transform="rotate(-90)"/>
            <circle r="80" fill="none" stroke="#12A594" stroke-width="44"
                    stroke-dasharray="100.5 502.65" stroke-dashoffset="-402.2" transform="rotate(-90)"/>
            <text y="-4" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="10.5" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="2">A DAY'S</text>
            <text y="18" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Space Mono, monospace" font-size="10.5" fill="#5B4E75" letter-spacing="2">ENERGY</text>
          </g>

          <g font-family="Newsreader, serif" font-size="17" fill="#241B36">
            <rect x="320" y="58" width="16" height="16" rx="4" fill="#F0A202"/>
            <text x="348" y="72">Carbohydrate — 50 to 60%</text>
            <text x="348" y="94" font-size="14.5" fill="#5B4E75">Rice, roti, oats, fruit, potatoes</text>

            <rect x="320" y="128" width="16" height="16" rx="4" fill="#372755"/>
            <text x="348" y="142">Fat — 20 to 30%</text>
            <text x="348" y="164" font-size="14.5" fill="#5B4E75">Oils, nuts, seeds, dairy</text>

            <rect x="320" y="198" width="16" height="16" rx="4" fill="#12A594"/>
            <text x="348" y="212">Protein — the rest</text>
            <text x="348" y="234" font-size="14.5" fill="#5B4E75">Dal, eggs, paneer, fish, soya</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>These are ranges, not rules. The <em>kind</em> of carbohydrate and the <em>kind</em> of fat matter far more than hitting an exact percentage.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Macro 01 — protein</p>
          <h4>Protects your muscle</h4>
          <p>When you eat less, your body burns some muscle along with fat. That is bad news, because muscle is what keeps your engine running fast.</p>
          <p>Enough protein tells the body: <em>burn the fat, leave the muscle alone</em>. It also keeps you fullest of the three.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Macro 02 — fat</p>
          <h4>Small amounts, big punch</h4>
          <p>Fat carries more than double the energy of the other two per gram, so it is easy to overdo.</p>
          <p>But you cannot cut it out. You need fat to absorb vitamins A, D, E and K. Choose liquid oils over ghee, butter and fried food.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="num">Macro 03 — carbs</p>
          <h4>Fuel, plus a quiet job</h4>
          <p>Your brain runs mostly on glucose. If carbs run out, the body makes glucose by breaking down muscle.</p>
          <p>So enough carbohydrate helps <em>spare</em> your protein for repair instead of burning it as fuel.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>And then there is fibre</h3>
      <p>Fibre is the part of plant food your body cannot digest. It sounds useless. It is one of the most useful things on the plate — about <strong>20 to 35&nbsp;grams a day</strong> — and it works in two ways.</p>
      <p><strong>It adds bulk without adding energy.</strong> Same calories, far more food. Your stomach stretches, and a stretched stomach sends its own fullness signal — one that still works fine even when leptin does not.</p>

      <figure>
        <div class="plates">
          <div>
            <p class="plate-label">200 kcal of vegetables &amp; dal</p>
            <div class="blocks" id="vegBlocks"></div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="plate-label">200 kcal of fried snack</p>
            <div class="blocks" id="fryBlocks"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Each square is the same amount of energy. Same calories, very different amount of food in your stomach. This is what "energy density" means.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p><strong>It slows things down.</strong> Fibre makes food leave the stomach more slowly, so fullness lasts longer. It also flattens out blood sugar spikes — which means less insulin — which loops straight back to the very first messenger on our list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- ============ 7. MYTHS ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">07 — Corrections</p>
      <h2>Four things you will hear that need a second look</h2>

      <div class="myth">
        <div class="myth-head"><span class="myth-num">01</span><h3>"It must be my thyroid."</h3></div>
        <p class="said">Usually not.</p>
        <p>Thyroid hormone levels are normally <em>normal</em> in people with obesity. A genuinely underactive thyroid can add a few kilos, mostly water, but it does not explain large weight gain. Worth testing, rarely the answer.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="myth">
        <div class="myth-head"><span class="myth-num">02</span><h3>"Limit saturated fat to 6–8% of your fat energy."</h3></div>
        <p class="said">Almost certainly a typo — check any slide that says this.</p>
        <p>Every real guideline says a percentage of <strong>total</strong> energy, not of fat energy. The current figure is under 10% of your total calories. Read literally, the version above would work out near 2%, which nobody could actually eat.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="myth">
        <div class="myth-head"><span class="myth-num">03</span><h3>"0.8 g of protein per kg is enough to protect muscle."</h3></div>
        <p class="said">That is the number for people who are <em>not</em> dieting.</p>
        <p>When you are eating less, the evidence points higher — roughly 1.2 to 1.6&nbsp;g per kg — to hold on to muscle. Not every study agrees, but that is where the weight of it sits.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="myth">
        <div class="myth-head"><span class="myth-num">04</span><h3>"Low-fat beats low-carb."</h3></div>
        <p class="said">It is basically a tie.</p>
        <p>A year-long trial with over 600 people found the two produced about the same weight loss. Bigger reviews give low-carb a small edge on weight and triglycerides, and low-fat a small edge on LDL cholesterol. The diet you can actually keep doing is the one that wins.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CLOSING ============ -->
  <section class="close">
    <div class="wrap rv">
      <p class="eyebrow">The one thing to remember</p>
      <h2>Your body is not disobeying you</h2>
      <p>It is defending you, using rules written long before food was easy to find. Storage full, gauge broken, hunger on, engine slowed — every step is a survival instinct doing its job in a world it was not built for.</p>
      <div class="pull">Knowing the signal is broken does not fix it. But it changes what you blame — and that turns out to matter quite a lot.</div>
      <p>And it points at what actually helps: enough protein to keep your engine, enough fibre to feel full through a signal that still works, and food you can keep eating next month. Not a fight against your body. A workaround.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <div class="wrap">
      <strong>WHERE THIS COMES FROM</strong><br>
      Leptin and leptin resistance: M Maffei et al. 1995 · Margetic et al. 2002 · Myers &amp; Allison 2014.<br>
      Saturated fat limit: Dietary Guidelines for Americans, 2025–2030 (under 10% of total calories).<br>
      Protein during energy restriction: systematic reviews supporting 1.2–1.6 g/kg/day for lean-mass retention.<br>
      Low-carb vs low-fat: DIETFITS randomised trial, 2018 · pooled analysis of 38 trials, 6,499 adults.<br><br>
      <strong>A NOTE</strong><br>
      This is general information for learning, not medical advice, and not a plan for any one person.<br>
      Anyone making real changes to how they eat should talk to a doctor or a registered dietitian first.<br>
      Numbers in the charts are illustrative and drawn to show direction and scale, not exact values.<br>
      If you are studying this in India, cross-check the macro ranges against ICMR–NIN guidelines,<br>
      which differ from the USDA figures used on most slides.
    </div>
  </footer>

</div>

